body:
The new staged code uses an additive schema layered on top of the existing `gate-state.json` shape:

```json
{
  "cleared_gates": {
    "pre_impl": true,
    "pre_merge": true
  },
  "gate_events": [
    {"gate": "pre_impl", "stage": "pre_impl", "cleared_at": "2026-05-04T10:00:00Z", "cleared_by": "operator"}
  ],
  "pending_stage": "pre_merge"
}
```

The legacy `cleared_gates` field is preserved verbatim and continues to be written by the new code (so old readers continue to work). Two new optional fields layer on top:

- `gate_events` (list of `{gate, stage, cleared_at, cleared_by}`): per-clearance audit trail.
- `pending_stage` (string | null): which lifecycle stage is currently waiting. `null` when the run is complete or no stage is currently pending.

Reason: minimum-blast-radius migration. Existing `gate-state.json` files in flight don't need to be rewritten or migrated. New readers can use `gate_events` + `pending_stage` for staged logic; old readers ignore the new fields (JSON parse tolerates extra keys). Plans started under the old upfront regime continue to work — their `cleared_gates: {pre_impl: true, pre_merge: true}` is read by the new staged code as 'both stages pre-cleared'.

How to apply: F001 step 5 introduces `load_gate_state_compat()` which returns a normalized object regardless of input shape. Acceptance #6 (legacy shape) and #7 (new shape) lock the round-trip behavior. #14 explicitly tests backwards compat for in-flight plans.
